# What must not enter a build context.
#
# The first two entries are the ones that matter. `COPY . .` in the builder
# stage copies whatever is in the context, so a local .env -- which holds the
# database password -- was being baked into a build layer. The final stage takes
# only the binary, so it never reached a published image, but a build layer is
# still a copy of the secret sitting in the daemon's cache.
#
# The rest is weight: the committed Windows binaries alone are ~28 MB of context
# uploaded on every build for a Linux image that cannot run them.

# Secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
deploy/.env.production
deploy/.env.production.local

# Build outputs and dumps
*.exe
*.exe~
*.test
*.out
access-terminal-cloud-api
deploy/backups/
*.dump

# Not used by the build
.git
.gitignore
.claude/
.vscode/
.idea/
.integration-run
docs/
*.md
!README.md

# The operator console. It is a separate deployable, served as static files from
# its own origin, and the Go binary neither embeds nor serves it -- so it has no
# business in this build context. node_modules alone would be hundreds of
# megabytes uploaded to the daemon on every image build.
web/
